#{[17592186549626 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 28 \\\\ -14 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186549637 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186549644 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 14 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186549654 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 14 & -28 \\\\ 14 & 14 & -28\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186549678 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186549714 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186549715 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186549745 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 14 \\\\ -28 & 28 \\\\ 14 & -28\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186549770 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186549780 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186549790 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186549924 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=2(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$760$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-40$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186549924 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)=2{y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}(2{y}^{T}y)=4y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=4{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i}.\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =4{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =4{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $4$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x,$ so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=4y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =4y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 & 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4(\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 & 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}76 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 & 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}760 & 760 \\\\ -40 & -40\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186549937 :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:V\\to W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x\\in V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$"] [17592186549937 :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186549952 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)={y}^{T}Ax$ where \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186549952 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 14 & -28 \\\\ 14 & 14 & -28\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186549972 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=2({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186549972 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}2{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=2{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=2(y\\cdot {x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix})=2(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 14 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 28 & -14 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186549979 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 2v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & v \\\\ 2v & u\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{v}^{2},\\,-u,\\,2v⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,xz⟩{|}_{x=u\\,y={v}^{2}\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}v⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u,\\,{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{v}^{2},\\,-u,\\,2v⟩ \\\\  & =u\\cdot (-2{v}^{2})+{v}^{2}\\cdot (-u)+{u}^{2}v\\cdot 2v \\\\  & =-2u{v}^{2}-u{v}^{2}+2{u}^{2}{v}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}{v}^{2}-3u{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\cdot {u}^{2}{v}^{2}+(-3)\\cdot u{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=2-3=-1.\n$$"] [17592186549986 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-23 & -15 \\\\ 9 & 19\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$"] [17592186549991 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)={y}^{T}Ax$ where \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186549991 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186550002 :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,9{x}_{1}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2}-50{x}_{1}+25{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=9{x}_{1}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $25{y}_{1}^{2}$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$"] [17592186550002 :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,\\underbrace{9{x}_{1}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2}}_{Q(x)}-50{x}_{1}+25{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2}-50{x}_{1}+25{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}-50(\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{1})+25(\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{2}) & =0 \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+50{y}_{1}-25{y}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}_{1}^{2}+50{y}_{1}-25{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1}+1-1-{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{1}+1)^{2}-1-{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & ={y}_{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}+1, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & ={y}_{2}+1 \\\\ {z}_{1}^{2} & ={z}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${z}_{2}$-axis."] [17592186550021 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+8y=-28 \\\\ 2x+3y=11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}},\n$$ where ${D}_{x}=$"] [17592186550021 :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+8y=-28 \\\\ 2x+3y=11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\,-28 & 8 \\\\ \\,\\,11 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}.\n$$"] [17592186550250 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{x y},\\,v=4 x^{3} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{2}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{2}{5 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=5 & ⟹\\, & u=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=4 x^{3} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{4 x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 4 x^{3} y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{7}{4 x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 4 x^{3} y=7 & ⟹\\, & v=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 5,\\,1\\le v\\le 7\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/d3/d3dd7067bbcb0a6e3c901d12a2795500e192594a14e7f1373dd325e4fd270363.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{2}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 12 x^{2} y & 4 x^{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{16 x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{16 x}=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{16 x^{3} y}=\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{2}^{5}\\int_{1}^{7}\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\int_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{1}^{7}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =(- \\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (\\ln 7-\\ln 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186550274 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{x y},\\,v=2 x^{3} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{2}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{2}{x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{3 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=6 & ⟹\\, & u=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=2 x^{3} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 2 x^{3} y=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{3}{x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 2 x^{3} y=6 & ⟹\\, & v=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 6,\\,2\\le v\\le 6\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/15/15f5caab85f05d666bb383692bad6b67d04da853f1412505a62ba6aba7ce36d6.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{2}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 6 x^{2} y & 2 x^{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{8 x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{8 x}=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{8 x^{3} y}=\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{6}\\int_{2}^{6}\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{6}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{2}^{6}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =(- \\frac{1}{6}+1)\\cdot (\\ln 6-\\ln 2) \\\\  & =\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186550281 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x^{2}},\\,v=x y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{y}{x^{2}}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=3 x^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{y}{x^{2}}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3 \\\\  & y=7 x^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{y}{x^{2}}=7 & ⟹\\, & u=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=x y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x} & ⟹\\, & x y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{8}{x} & ⟹\\, & x y=8 & ⟹\\, & v=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 7,\\,1\\le v\\le 8\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/6c/6cd1c5b38643b6c5e0ceded7b3cae3d06e51135f84fb9598a129e8627b55c14a.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2 y}{x^{3}} & \\frac{1}{x^{2}} \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =- \\frac{3 y}{x^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=- \\frac{x^{2}}{3 y}=- \\frac{1}{3 u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|- \\frac{1}{3 u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{3}^{7}\\int_{1}^{8}\\frac{1}{3 u}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{3}^{7}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{1}^{8}1\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\ln 7-\\ln 3)\\cdot (8-1) \\\\  & =- \\frac{7 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{7 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186550282 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{3}{x y},\\,v=3 x^{3} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{3}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{3}{x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{3}{x y}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{3}{8 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{3}{x y}=8 & ⟹\\, & u=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=3 x^{3} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 3 x^{3} y=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3 \\\\  & y=\\frac{5}{3 x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 3 x^{3} y=5 & ⟹\\, & v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 8,\\,3\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/80/80a923224b455c6f8cfa17a7099c1bb86206a115845954af6123bfb6b3ef5138.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{3}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{3}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 9 x^{2} y & 3 x^{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{18 x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{18 x}=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{18 x^{3} y}=\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{8}\\int_{3}^{5}\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int_{1}^{8}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{3}^{5}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (- \\frac{1}{8}+1)\\cdot (\\ln 5-\\ln 3) \\\\  & =- \\frac{21 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{16} + \\frac{21 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186550289 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\,v=4 x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=- 2 x + y$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2 x - 2 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=-2 & ⟹\\, & u=-2 \\\\  & y=2 x + 2 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=4 x + y$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2 - 4 x & ⟹\\, & 4 x + y=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2 \\\\  & y=4 - 4 x & ⟹\\, & 4 x + y=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/43/434447c0d9ece418e17a0ac3f0cc044faf76ba5689ed692fc3408cb78501326c.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{6}=- \\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|- \\frac{1}{6}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186550292 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{x y},\\,v=x^{2} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{3 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{x y}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{8 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{x y}=8 & ⟹\\, & u=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=x^{2} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x^{2}} & ⟹\\, & x^{2} y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{4}{x^{2}} & ⟹\\, & x^{2} y=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 8,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/f5/f506bb81b18dce423e50eedc19841b7a5896230413f7f1a876aa93d27e68edf9.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{1}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{1}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 2 x y & x^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=y=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{x^{2} y}=\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{3}^{8}\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\int_{3}^{8}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =(- \\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot (\\ln 4-\\ln 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186550299 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\,v=x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=- 2 x + y$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2 x & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=0 & ⟹\\, & u=0 \\\\  & y=2 x + 2 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=x + y$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=3 - x & ⟹\\, & x + y=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3 \\\\  & y=4 - x & ⟹\\, & x + y=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,3\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/2c/2c3cb5e8b7c7448b5f90e372e20e6ff2bd0e547dd085d34ac3103178083ce8c2.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{3}=- \\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|- \\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186550308 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{x y},\\,v=4 x^{3} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{2}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{2}{3 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{4 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=8 & ⟹\\, & u=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=4 x^{3} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{4 x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 4 x^{3} y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 4 x^{3} y=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 8,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/f5/f506bb81b18dce423e50eedc19841b7a5896230413f7f1a876aa93d27e68edf9.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{2}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 12 x^{2} y & 4 x^{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{16 x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{16 x}=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{16 x^{3} y}=\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{3}^{8}\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\int_{3}^{8}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =(- \\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot (\\ln 4-\\ln 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186550309 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{3}{x y},\\,v=2 x^{3} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{3}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{3}{4 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{3}{x y}=4 & ⟹\\, & u=4 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{3 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{3}{x y}=9 & ⟹\\, & u=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=2 x^{3} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 2 x^{3} y=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{7}{2 x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 2 x^{3} y=7 & ⟹\\, & v=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,4\\le u\\le 9,\\,2\\le v\\le 7\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/07/075e4196e1ad4b6dafc70878555598bac64e8880b2c152d61c2a6437acbaaceb.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{3}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{3}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 6 x^{2} y & 2 x^{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{12 x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{12 x}=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{12 x^{3} y}=\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{4}^{9}\\int_{2}^{7}\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int_{4}^{9}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{2}^{7}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (- \\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot (\\ln 7-\\ln 2) \\\\  & =- \\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{24} + \\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186550311 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\,v=3 x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=- 2 x + y$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2 x - 4 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=-4 & ⟹\\, & u=-4 \\\\  & y=2 x + 4 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=4 & ⟹\\, & u=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=3 x + y$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=3 - 3 x & ⟹\\, & 3 x + y=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3 \\\\  & y=5 - 3 x & ⟹\\, & 3 x + y=5 & ⟹\\, & v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 4,\\,3\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/43/438f7c0ee42f63a596ff65ae9c2b354c93bb2f97ad4d7b5b0a1a161a3c5d173b.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{5}=- \\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|- \\frac{1}{5}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 8\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186550313 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\,v=4 x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=- 2 x + y$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2 x & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=0 & ⟹\\, & u=0 \\\\  & y=2 x + 3 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=4 x + y$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=1 - 4 x & ⟹\\, & 4 x + y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=5 - 4 x & ⟹\\, & 4 x + y=5 & ⟹\\, & v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/d6/d605500af69b1a828496810a93a084b065ec92ebd99b03cbf14a37b03840dd31.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{6}=- \\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|- \\frac{1}{6}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186550378 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550381 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 8\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186550397 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550398 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550402 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0.376 \\\\ 0.624\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550408 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 & -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550413 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186550417 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550427 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550428 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0.333 \\\\ 0.667\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550446 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550447 :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\